Question: Determine whether the following two code snippets are semantic code clones.

Code 1: Python
```python
n = int(input())
total = 0
for i in range(1, n + 1):
    if i % 3 == 0 or i % 5 == 0:
        total += i
print(total)
```

Code 2: Rust
```rust
use std::io;

fn main() {
    let mut buf = String::new();
    io::stdin().read_line(&mut buf).unwrap();
    let n: i64 = buf.trim().parse().unwrap();
    let mut total: i64 = 0;
    for i in 1..=n {
        if i % 3 == 0 || i % 5 == 0 {
            total += i;
        }
    }
    println!("{}", total);
}
```
<analysis>
Code 1 reads one integer n from standard input and prints the sum of the values in 1..n divisible by 3 or by 5.<end_of_step>

Code 2 reads one integer n from standard input and prints the same sum. Both sides are complete stdin-to-stdout programs.<end_of_step>

The loop bounds are easy to misread, so rather than predict Code 2's output, run both programs on the same inputs and compare what they actually print. A match on one small input would be weak evidence on its own, so test a boundary case, a small ordinary case, and a larger case before deciding, per rule 13. Keep each step short so nothing is cut off.<end_of_step>
<end_of_analysis>

<code>
import subprocess
def run_both(s):
    py = subprocess.run(["python3", "candidate_code.py"], input=s, capture_output=True, text=True)
    rs = subprocess.run(["./candidate_code2"], input=s, capture_output=True, text=True)
    return py.stdout.strip(), rs.stdout.strip()
<end_of_step>

# Boundary case: smallest positive n, no multiples of 3 or 5 below it.
out1, out2 = run_both("1")
assert out1 == out2, (out1, out2)
<end_of_step>

# Small ordinary case.
out1, out2 = run_both("17")
assert out1 == out2, (out1, out2)
<end_of_step>

# Larger case, structurally different in scale from the two above.
out1, out2 = run_both("10000")
assert out1 == out2, (out1, out2)
print("Both programs printed the same output on a boundary, a small, and a large input.")
<end_of_code>
<output>
