Code 2 (Rust) compiled to ./candidate_code2: run it with subprocess.run(['./candidate_code2'], input=..., capture_output=True, text=True).
<end_of_output><answer>
Final decision: \boxed{clone}
Reason: Both programs were executed on a boundary input, an ordinary small input, and a much larger input, and printed identical output every time, so they compute the same function of standard input.
<end_of_answer>

Question: Determine whether the following two code snippets are semantic code clones.

Code 1: Python
```python
a = int(input())
b = int(input())
print(a // b)
```

Code 2: Rust
```rust
use std::io;

fn read() -> i64 {
    let mut buf = String::new();
    io::stdin().read_line(&mut buf).unwrap();
    buf.trim().parse().unwrap()
}

fn main() {
    let a = read();
    let b = read();
    println!("{}", a / b);
}
```
<analysis>
Code 1 reads two integers from standard input and prints their quotient.<end_of_step>

Code 2 reads two integers from standard input and prints their quotient, but Rust integer division truncates toward zero while Python's // floors.<end_of_step>

The two are textually near-identical, so this is a behavioural suspicion rather than a signature mismatch. That is exactly what running both sides settles, so test it instead of guessing. Keep each step short.<end_of_step>
<end_of_analysis>

<code>
import subprocess
def run_both(s):
    py = subprocess.run(["python3", "candidate_code.py"], input=s, capture_output=True, text=True)
    rs = subprocess.run(["./candidate_code2"], input=s, capture_output=True, text=True)
    return py.stdout.strip(), rs.stdout.strip()
<end_of_step>

out1, out2 = run_both("-7\n2")
print("Code 1:", out1, "| Code 2:", out2)
